# CareerVerse AI — Engineering Specification

Permanent rules for all AI-assisted development on this project.

---

## SOURCE OF TRUTH

Before generating or modifying code, always read:

- `README.md`
- `docs/PRD.md`
- `.cursorrules`

These three files are the project's single source of truth.

---

## PROJECT

CareerVerse AI is an AI-powered career guidance platform that helps students discover suitable career paths through AI-powered resume analysis, Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), career recommendations, interactive job simulations, skill gap analysis, and personalized learning roadmaps.

The platform recommends the **Top 3** most suitable careers based on the uploaded resume and uploaded Job Description PDFs. Users explore each recommendation, select one career to pursue, experience it through an AI-generated simulation, and receive skill gap analysis and a personalized learning roadmap grounded in that choice.

> Experience the role before choosing your future.

### Core User Flow

```
Resume Upload
        ↓
Resume Reviewer Agent
        ↓
Career Recommendation Agent
        ↓
Top 3 Career Recommendations
        ↓
User selects one career
        ↓
Simulation Agent
        ↓
Simulation Evaluation
        ↓
Skill Gap Agent
        ↓
Roadmap Agent
        ↓
Dashboard
```

**Career Mentor Chatbot** is available throughout the application and is **not** part of the sequential pipeline above.

### Scope Boundaries

- **MVP:** The end-to-end flow above must be functional. Build with mock/dummy data first; wire real AI and DB integrations last on each feature.
- **Downstream agents** (simulation evaluation, skill gap, roadmap) run only for the **single career the user selects** — not for all three recommendations.
- Career recommendations must always originate from uploaded Job Description PDFs via RAG — never hardcoded.
- Only the MVP contains three handcrafted job simulations for the three most common careers.
- Every downstream output must trace back to the **specific selected career's JD text** and the user's simulation performance, not generic role templates.

---

## TECH STACK

### Frontend
- React
- TypeScript
- Vite
- Tailwind CSS
- shadcn/ui
- Framer Motion

### Backend
- FastAPI

### Database
- PostgreSQL
- SQLAlchemy
- Alembic

### Authentication
- JWT (MVP). Clerk is a post-MVP swap — do not introduce Clerk during MVP unless explicitly requested.

### Storage
- Supabase Storage (resume and JD PDFs)

### AI
- OpenAI GPT-4o
- text-embedding-3-small embeddings

### Vector Database
- ChromaDB (local, persisted to disk for MVP)

### Resume Parsing
- PyMuPDF

### Deployment (reference)
- Frontend: Vercel
- Backend: Render
- Do not add deployment-specific code to business logic.

---

## PROJECT STRUCTURE

All new files must live inside this structure. Never create files outside it.

```
frontend/src/
    pages/
    components/
    hooks/
    services/
    contexts/
    lib/
    types/

backend/app/
    api/          # route handlers only
    agents/       # AI agent orchestration only
    prompts/      # per-agent prompt templates only
    services/     # business logic only
    rag/          # embedding + retrieval logic
    models/       # Pydantic schemas + SQLAlchemy models
    utils/
    core/         # config, security, dependencies
    main.py

docs/
uploads/
data/
vector_db/
tests/
```

---

## LAYER RESPONSIBILITIES

| Layer | Responsibility | Must NOT contain |
|---|---|---|
| `api/` | HTTP routes, request validation, response serialization | Business logic, AI calls, DB queries |
| `services/` | Business logic, orchestration, DB access | HTTP concerns, raw prompt strings |
| `agents/` | AI agent orchestration, calling OpenAI | HTTP routes, direct DB access |
| `prompts/` | Prompt templates (one file per agent) | Runtime logic, API code |
| `rag/` | Chunking, embedding, ChromaDB retrieval | Agent reasoning, HTTP routes |
| `models/` | Pydantic request/response schemas, SQLAlchemy ORM models | Business logic |
| `pages/` | Route-level screen composition | Direct API calls (use services/hooks) |
| `components/` | Reusable UI | Business logic |
| `hooks/` | Stateful UI logic, data fetching wrappers | Raw fetch URL strings scattered in pages |
| `services/` (frontend) | API client functions | React component code |

---

## AI Response Rules

-All AI outputs must return structured JSON whenever consumed by another agent or the frontend.

-Natural language responses should only be generated for user-facing interfaces such as the Career Mentor Chatbot and Job Simulation.

-Agents should never return free-form text when downstream processing depends on the output.

## ENGINEERING RULES

- Always read `README.md`, `docs/PRD.md`, and `.cursorrules` before implementing any feature.
- Never introduce a new dependency without explaining why.
- Never create files outside the project structure.
- Business logic belongs only inside `services/`.
- API routes belong only inside `api/`.
- AI logic belongs only inside `agents/`.
- Every AI agent must have its own prompt template in `backend/app/prompts/`.
- Every request and response must use Pydantic models.
- Prefer modular, reusable code.
- Prefer async FastAPI endpoints.
- Never leave TODOs in production code.
- Generate production-quality code only.
- Never hardcode career recommendations.
- Career recommendations must always come from uploaded Job Description PDFs using RAG.
- Only the MVP contains three handcrafted job simulations for the three most common careers.
- Agents must be independently testable — each agent is called on its own, not buried inside another agent's prompt.
- RAG retrieval must use embeddings from uploaded JD PDFs stored in ChromaDB — never hallucinate job requirements not present in retrieved context.
- Store parsed resume text and full matched JD text per recommendation so downstream agents can reuse them without re-parsing.
- Use environment variables for all secrets (`OPENAI_API_KEY`, `DATABASE_URL`, `SUPABASE_URL`, `SUPABASE_KEY`, `JWT_SECRET`). Never hardcode credentials.
- Handle loading states and AI timeouts gracefully — simulation and evaluation steps involve multiple AI calls; show progress to the user.

Development Strategy

Build the project incrementally.

Every feature must be completed, tested, and explained before moving to the next feature.

Never scaffold or implement multiple major features in one step.

Always prefer a working MVP over partially implemented advanced functionality.

---

## AI AGENT PIPELINE

Sequential pipeline (Career Mentor Chatbot is **not** part of this flow):

```
Resume Upload
        ↓
Resume Reviewer Agent
        ↓
Career Recommendation Agent  (RAG → Top 3 with match %, reasoning, missing skills, overview)
        ↓
Top 3 Career Recommendations
        ↓
User selects one career
        ↓
Simulation Agent  (workplace simulation for the selected career)
        ↓
Simulation Evaluation  (scores + personalized feedback)
        ↓
Skill Gap Agent  (resume + selected career + simulation performance)
        ↓
Roadmap Agent  (3-month plan from resume, career, gap analysis, simulation feedback)
        ↓
Dashboard
```

**Career Mentor Chatbot** — available throughout the application; not part of the sequential pipeline.

---

## AI AGENTS

### Resume Reviewer Agent
**Prompt file:** `backend/app/prompts/resume_reviewer.py`

**Responsibilities**
- Resume analysis
- Resume score
- ATS feedback
- Resume strengths
- Resume weaknesses

---

### Career Recommendation Agent
**Prompt file:** `backend/app/prompts/career_recommendation.py`

**Responsibilities**
- Retrieve relevant Job Descriptions using RAG
- Compare resume against retrieved JDs
- Recommend the Top 3 career matches
- Return match percentages
- Explain reasoning
- Return missing skills for each recommendation
- Return a short career overview

---

### Simulation Agent
**Prompt file:** `backend/app/prompts/simulation.py`

**Responsibilities**
- Generate realistic workplace simulations for the **selected career**, grounded in that career's JD text
- Support multiple career types
- Generate 5–10 conversational turns
- Evaluate user decisions
- Produce a Simulation Score
- Produce a Communication Score
- Produce a Problem Solving Score
- Produce personalized feedback

---

### Skill Gap Agent
**Prompt file:** `backend/app/prompts/skill_gap.py`

**Inputs**
- Resume
- Selected Career
- Simulation Performance

**Outputs**
- Missing Technical Skills
- Missing Soft Skills
- Improvement Suggestions

---

### Roadmap Agent
**Prompt file:** `backend/app/prompts/roadmap.py`

**Inputs**
- Resume
- Selected Career
- Skill Gap Analysis
- Simulation Feedback

**Responsibilities**
- Generate a personalized 3-month learning roadmap (Month 1 / Month 2 / Month 3, each with learning goals, topics, resources, and practice suggestions)

---

### Career Mentor Chatbot
**Prompt file:** `backend/app/prompts/career_mentor.py`

**Availability**
- Available throughout the application — not part of the sequential pipeline

**Responsibilities**
- Answer career questions using retrieved knowledge only
- Use RAG retrieval
- Use Resume Analysis
- Use Career Recommendations
- Use Skill Gap Analysis
- Use Simulation Feedback
- **Never hallucinate outside retrieved knowledge** — if context is insufficient, say so

---

## MVP SCREENS

Build these screens only unless explicitly asked to add more:

1. Landing Page
2. Login / Signup
3. Dashboard
4. Upload Resume
5. Resume Report (score, strengths, weaknesses, ATS feedback)
6. Upload Job Description
7. Top 3 Career Recommendations (match %, reasoning, missing skills, career overview per card)
8. Career Match Explorer (explore each recommendation; "Experience this Career" action)
9. Job Simulation (interactive simulation for the selected career)
10. Simulation Evaluation (simulation, communication, and problem-solving scores + feedback)
11. Skill Gap (missing technical/soft skills, improvement suggestions)
12. Learning Roadmap (3-month plan)
13. Final Dashboard (resume score, top matches, simulation results, skill gap summary, roadmap, mentor access)

---

## CODING STYLE

- Prefer readability over cleverness.
- Keep files focused and small.
- Use descriptive names.
- Reuse components whenever possible.
- Write code that is easy for students to understand.
- Explain architectural decisions when appropriate.
- Match existing naming conventions and import style in the file you are editing.
- Frontend: compose pages from small components; extract repeated UI into `components/`.
- Backend: one route handler per endpoint; delegate immediately to a service or agent.

---

## WHAT NOT TO DO

- Do not scaffold or add features not requested in the current task.
- Do not add Clerk, Pinecone, or other post-MVP dependencies unless explicitly requested.
- Do not put prompt strings inline in agent or service files — always import from `prompts/`.
- Do not hardcode career recommendations — all recommendations must come from RAG over uploaded JD PDFs.
- Do not run simulation, skill gap, or roadmap agents for all 3 matches — only for the user's selected career.
- Do not generate generic simulations disconnected from the selected career's JD content.
- Do not skip Pydantic validation on any API boundary.
- Do not let the Career Mentor Chatbot answer questions outside retrieved knowledge or generated analysis.
